  "body": "What happens (main 5fdbed9bd, after #2596's fix in PR #2638):\n\nA 2D Chamfer with no picks, on a 60 x 40 x 10 plate that has a 1 mm modelled bevel on the X0 side only, plus a sharp 20 x 10 pocket:\n\n- The generator classifies each top loop whole (`crates/motion/src/machining2d/chamfer_ride.rs:68-69`, one-sided vertex `gap`, line 113). The outer loop counts as bevelled, so all four outer sides are ridden, and the three sharp sides (Y0, Y40, X60) are cut at z 9.2.\n- The verifier then refuses the op: `models_a_bevel` (`crates/sim/src/stock_sim/wall_anchor.rs:63-71`) fires on any bevel sample, and `crates/app/src/stock_sim_breaks.rs:144` drops all of the op's edge-break credit. Gate: `gouge: program cuts below the model in 840 cell(s), worst 0.74 mm at (1.31, 39.94, z 9.26)`.\n- Without the extra collinear vertex on the bevel's top edge, the outer loop reads as sharp (gap 0, `chamfer_ride.rs:68`): the tip rides x = 1 inside the part, and the pocket rim is ridden too. Verify again refuses (1147 cells, 0.74 mm).\n\nSo the output is safe: no posted program cuts into a sharp edge. But the user gets a gouge line at a coordinate, not a reason. The op cannot be used without picking the edges, and nothing says that.\n\nExpected:\n- With no picks, ride only the modelled bevels, per segment rather than per whole loop, OR refuse at generation with a message that names the partly bevelled loop and asks for picks.\n- Classify bevel vs sharp in a way that does not depend on tessellation (two-sided gap, or per segment).\n- A note names the edges the op left sharp.\n\nFound by: a blind safety review in a model trial (probes built on wopr against main; no code from the trial is proposed). Related: #2596, PR #2638.\n\nDone means: the 60 x 40 plate with a bevel on one side posts a clean chamfer of that side only (or a named refusal that asks for picks), with and without the extra vertex; a pinning test for both; no change to the all-sharp-part behaviour (#303) or to the verifier's refusal of cuts into sharp edges.\n",
